.TH GIT-STAGE-BATCH-FIXUP 1 "August 2026" "git-stage-batch @VERSION@" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
git-stage-batch-fixup \- inspect fixup targets or create grouped fixup commits
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B git-stage-batch fixup suggest
[\fB\-\-line\fR \fIIDS\fR]
[\fB\-\-reset\fR|\fB\-\-abort\fR|\fB\-\-last\fR]
[\fB\-\-porcelain\fR]
[\fIBOUNDARY\fR]
.PP
.B git-stage-batch fixup create
[\fB\-\-plan\fR \fIFILE\fR]
[\fB\-\-dry-run\fR]
[\fB\-\-partial\fR]
[\fB\-\-porcelain\fR]
[\fIBOUNDARY\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B fixup suggest
is the namespaced form of
.BR suggest-fixup .
It preserves disjoint source ranges, handles additions through adjacent-line
anchors, and reports exact-line lineage separately from tree-replay placement.
.PP
.B fixup create
analyzes the exact staged index, partitions eligible textual change units by
target commit, and creates one ordinary
.B fixup!
commit per target. It does not stage unstaged changes, rewrite existing
commits, run autosquash, or publish anything.
.PP
Attribution combines exact-line history with tree-replay commutation. The
command creates fixups only when those signals agree, or when unique line
history chooses a target and the patch commutes through the complete range.
Ambiguous, conflicting, unsupported, and placement-only units remain staged.
.SH ACTIONS
.TP
.B suggest
Suggest targets for the selected hunk or selected line IDs. Repeated
invocations walk backward through candidates. This action does not create a
commit or change the real index.
.TP
.B create
Plan or create grouped fixup commits from staged changes.
.SH CREATE OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-plan " FILE"
Read reviewed assignments from reusable JSON emitted by
.BR "fixup create --dry-run --porcelain" .
The plan freezes its boundary, so this option cannot be combined with
.IR BOUNDARY .
.TP
.B \-\-dry-run
Print the complete unit assignment plan without creating commits or updating
refs.
.TP
.B \-\-partial
Create fixup commits for eligible units even when other units cannot be
assigned. Without this option, any remaining unit prevents all mutation.
.TP
.B \-\-porcelain
Print versioned JSON containing the frozen range, source trees, per-unit
evidence, reviewed assignments, target groups, created commits, and recovery
ref. Dry-run JSON can be saved and passed back with
.BR \-\-plan .
.TP
.I BOUNDARY
Commit excluded from the target range. By default the command uses the fork
point, or merge base, between
.B HEAD
and its configured upstream.
.SH SAFETY
The target range must be non-empty and linear. Plans are bound to full commit
and tree object IDs and are rejected if HEAD or the index changes during the
operation.
.PP
Only the assignment array is editable plan input. Automatic assignments may
be changed to explicit assignments after semantic review. Explicit assignments
remain subject to tree-replay placement: they cannot cross a barrier or an
unknown transition. Plan replay regenerates every range and unit fact, rejects
omission or duplication, validates grouped patch conservation, and replays
the groups at their targets before creating commits.
.PP
Fixup commits are materialized in isolated indexes; the real index and
unstaged worktree remain untouched. On success, unresolved units remain
staged. A private recovery ref records the original HEAD and is printed in
command output.
.PP
Each generated fixup body contains a random verification marker. The command
uses it to avoid overwriting HEAD if a hook moves the ref after commit
creation. Autosquash discards this marker with the rest of the fixup body.
.PP
Commit hooks inherit the isolated
.B GIT_INDEX_FILE
used to materialize the proposed fixup tree. Hook reads and writes therefore
target the temporary index rather than the user's real staged index. After a
hook returns, the command checks the marker, parent, subject, and complete
tree, and safely rolls back an unexpected result when HEAD is still owned by
the operation. Hooks must not assume that
.B GIT_INDEX_FILE
names the ordinary index.
.PP
Successful creation deliberately retains its recovery ref below
.BR refs/git-stage-batch/fixup/backups/ .
The command does not prune those refs because each one keeps the original tip
reachable for recovery. Inspect accumulated refs with:
.PP
.B git for-each-ref --format='%(refname) %(objectname)' refs/git-stage-batch/fixup/backups/
.PP
After recovery is no longer useful, delete a reviewed ref with an exact
expected-old-value check:
.PP
.B git update-ref -d EXACT_RECOVERY_REF EXPECTED_ORIGINAL_HEAD
.PP
Deleting the ref removes the recovery anchor and may allow the original
commits to be garbage-collected.
.PP
Generated fixup titles use the readable target subject unless autosquash
subject matching would select another commit. In that case, the title uses
the full target object ID.
.PP
An active git-stage-batch session or an in-progress merge, cherry-pick,
revert, rebase, sequencer, or bisect operation prevents commit creation.
Dry-run analysis remains available.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR git-stage-batch (1),
.BR git-stage-batch-suggest-fixup (1),
.BR git-commit (1),
.BR git-rebase (1)
